 kit people - lists who has access to the repository, its teams, and the bots that acted

   one of
   *kit people*  lists who has access to the repository, its teams, and the bots that acted

     --text   --json   --jq
                           prints the stages as text, even on a terminal

   --stages    prints the stages, one per line, in order, the same over any workspace
   --marks     prints a mark per stage, as one JSON object: null for each, as each is read again
               only on Refresh and Watch
   people      {login, name, role, kind}, keyed by the login: name null where the forge shows none;
               role the forge's own name for it, as GitHub's admin, write, or read, GitLab's owner,
               developer, or guest, and on Azure DevOps admin for a team's administrator, else
               member; kind user, bot, or, on Azure DevOps, group. GitHub shows it only to who may
               push, and a caller who may not is told so. GitLab names its members, those inherited
               and invited among them, each at the highest role any gives them; Azure DevOps the
               members of the project's teams, as teams reads them
   teams       {team, name, role, members}, keyed by the team, GitHub's slug, GitLab's group path,
               Azure DevOps' name: role the access the team has, null on Azure DevOps; members their
               logins, null for a team the caller cannot see. The team links to its page. GitHub's
               need the read:org scope; GitLab's are the groups the project is shared with. Past 20
               teams, or past 100 members of one, the document is limited, at the teams drawn
   agents      {login, acted, last}, keyed by the login: acted what it did, commit, change, or
               review; last when it last did, in seconds since the epoch. The login links to its
               page. GitHub reads the bots among the authors of the 100 newest commits on the
               default branch, and of the open changes and the 50 merged last, 100 open at most, and
               their reviews, in one request: a bot is a Bot account, or a login that ends [bot];
               past 100 open changes, or 20 reviewers of one, the document is limited, at the bots
               drawn. GitLab's are its bot users among the members, acted null, last the day each
               last acted. Azure DevOps names no bots, and draws none


 kit people
